[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
**Mapbox GL JS (v5-style)**  

```js
import mapboxgl from 'mapbox-gl';
```

**MapLibre GL JS v6 equivalent**

```js
// ✅ Namespace import – gives you the same `maplibregl` object you used to get from Mapbox
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

// or, if you only need the `Map` class (and maybe a few other symbols)
import { Map, NavigationControl } from 'maplibre-gl';
```

### Why the change?

* **v6 removed the default export** – `import mapboxgl from 'mapbox-gl'` (or `import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl'`) no longer works.
* Use a **named import** (`{ Map, … }`) or a **namespace import** (`* as maplibregl`) instead.
* Pin the version you’re loading (e.g. `npm i maplibre-gl@^6.0.0` or `https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.0.0/...`) so a future major release doesn’t silently break the import.

Now you can create a map just like before:

```js
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',          // id of the DOM element
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});
```

**Important note – v6 no longer ships a CommonJS entry point**  
Starting with **MapLibre GL JS v6** the package’s `package.json` only exports an **ESM** build (`"import"` condition). A plain

```js
const maplibregl = require('maplibre-gl');
```

will throw `ERR_PACKAGE_PATH_NOT_EXPORTED`.  
You have three practical ways to keep a **CommonJS** script:

1. **Pin to v5** (the last version that still supports `require`).  
2. **Read the version from the package’s `package.json`** – this works because JSON files are still CommonJS-loadable.  
3. **Use a dynamic `import()`** inside the CommonJS file (Node ≥ 14). This gives you the real library object and works with v6.

Below are the three snippets. Choose the one that matches what you need.

---

## 1️⃣ Pin to v5 (still works with `require`)

```bash
# Install the last v5 release
npm i maplibre-gl@5
```

```js
// file: print-maplibre-version.cjs
const maplibregl = require('maplibre-gl');

console.log('MapLibre GL JS version:', maplibregl.version);
```

Run:

```bash
node print-maplibre-version.cjs
```

---

## 2️⃣ Read the version from `package.json` (works for any installed version)

```js
// file: print-maplibre-version.cjs
// No need to load the library itself – just read its package.json
const { version } = require('maplibre-gl/package.json');

console.log('MapLibre GL JS version (from package.json):', version);
```

Run the same way:

```bash
node print-maplibre-version.cjs
```

*Pros*: works with v6 (or any future major) without any ESM interop.  
*Cons*: you don’t get the library object; you only know the installed version.

---

## 3️⃣ Dynamic `import()` – stay in a CommonJS file but load the ESM build

```js
// file: print-maplibre-version.cjs
(async () => {
  try {
    // Dynamic import returns the ESM namespace object
    const maplibregl = await import('maplibre-gl');

    // v6 still exposes a static `version` property on the namespace
    // (some older snippets used `default.version`; both are covered)
    const version = maplibregl.version ?? maplibregl.default?.version;

    console.log('MapLibre GL JS version (dynamic import):', version);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Failed to load maplibre-gl:', err);
    process.exit(1);
  }
})();
```

Run:

```bash
node print-maplibre-version.cjs
```

*Why this works*:  
- The file itself is **CommonJS** (`.cjs` or `type: "commonjs"` in `package.json`).  
- `import()` is a native promise-based loader that can bring in an ESM module from a CommonJS context.  
- No bundler is involved, so you don’t need `setWorkerUrl()`.

---

### Quick checklist

| What you need | Which snippet to use |
|---------------|----------------------|
| Keep using `require` unchanged → stay on v5 | **#1** |
| Only need the version number, no library code | **#2** |
| Want the real library (v6+) while staying in a CommonJS file | **#3** |

---

#### TL;DR

```js
// print-maplibre-version.cjs  (dynamic import – works with v6)
(async () => {
  const { version } = await import('maplibre-gl')
    .then(m => ({ version: m.version ?? m.default?.version }));
  console.log('MapLibre GL JS version:', version);
})();
```

That’s the minimal, future-proof way to print the version from a **CommonJS** script after the v5→v6 migration. Happy mapping!

Below is the **v6-compatible** way to supply an icon that isn’t present in the style’s sprite sheet.

---

## 1. What changed?

| v5 (still works in v5) | v6 (required) |
|------------------------|----------------|
| Listen for `styleimagemissing` and call `map.addImage(id, …)` **inside the handler**. The map will automatically resolve the pending request. | `styleimagemissing` is now **notify-only** – calling `addImage` from the handler does **not** resolve the request. You must register a **resolver** with `map.setMissingStyleImageResolver()` that returns the image (or a `Promise` that resolves to it). |

> **TL;DR** – keep the `styleimagemissing` listener only for debugging/telemetry; the real work is done by the resolver.

---

## 2. Basic synchronous resolver

If you can generate the image synchronously (e.g. from a canvas, an in-memory `ImageBitmap`, or a pre-loaded `HTMLImageElement`), just return it:

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';   // v6 – namespace import
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';

const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://example.com/style.json',
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2,
});

/**
 * Called whenever the style references an icon name that is not in the sprite.
 * `id` is the exact string used in `icon-image`.
 *
 * Return one of:
 *   • HTMLImageElement
 *   • ImageBitmap
 *   • CanvasImageSource (canvas, video, etc.)
 *   • { width, height, data: Uint8Array } (raw RGBA)
 *
 * Throwing or returning `null/undefined` tells the map “I don’t have it”.
 */
map.setMissingStyleImageResolver((id) => {
  // Example: generate a simple colored circle on a canvas
  const size = 64;
  const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');
  canvas.width = canvas.height = size;
  const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');

  // background (transparent)
  ctx.clearRect(0, 0, size, size);

  // draw a circle with a colour derived from the id (just for demo)
  ctx.beginPath();
  ctx.arc(size / 2, size / 2, size / 2 - 4, 0, Math.PI * 2);
  ctx.fillStyle = `hsl(${hashStringToHue(id)}, 70%, 50%)`;
  ctx.fill();

  // optional: draw the id text
  ctx.font = '12px sans-serif';
  ctx.fillStyle = '#fff';
  ctx.textAlign = 'center';
  ctx.textBaseline = 'middle';
  ctx.fillText(id[0].toUpperCase(), size / 2, size / 2);

  // The canvas itself is a valid `CanvasImageSource`
  return canvas;
});

/* Helper – deterministic hue from a string */
function hashStringToHue(str) {
  let hash = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < str.length; i++) hash = (hash * 31 + str.charCodeAt(i)) >>> 0;
  return (hash % 360);
}
```

**Result:** Whenever a layer asks for `icon-image: 'my-dynamic-icon'` and that name isn’t in the sprite, the resolver runs, creates a canvas, and the map automatically adds it as an image with the same `id`. No extra `map.addImage()` call is needed.

---

## 3. Asynchronous resolver (fetching from a server)

If the icon must be fetched (e.g. from a CDN, an API, or generated on a worker), return a **Promise** that resolves to one of the accepted image sources.

```js
map.setMissingStyleImageResolver(async (id) => {
  // Example: fetch a PNG from a custom endpoint
  const response = await fetch(`https://my-icons.example.com/${encodeURIComponent(id)}.png`);
  if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Icon not found');

  // Convert the blob to an ImageBitmap (fast for WebGL)
  const blob = await response.blob();
  const bitmap = await createImageBitmap(blob);
  return bitmap;               // ← resolves the request
});
```

You can also return a plain `HTMLImageElement` if you prefer:

```js
map.setMissingStyleImageResolver((id) => {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const img = new Image();
    img.crossOrigin = 'anonymous';
    img.onload = () => resolve(img);
    img.onerror = reject;
    img.src = `https://my-icons.example.com/${encodeURIComponent(id)}.svg`;
  });
});
```

**Important:**  
* The promise **must resolve** (or reject) *before* the map tries to render the symbol that needs the icon. The map will automatically wait for the promise, so you don’t need any extra `await` logic elsewhere.  
* If the promise rejects, the map will treat the icon as missing and fall back to the `icon-allow-overlap`/`icon-ignore-placement` rules (or the default “missing-icon” placeholder).

---

## 4. Optional: keep `styleimagemissing` for logging

You can still listen to the event if you want to know *when* the resolver is invoked:

```js
map.on('styleimagemissing', (e) => {
  console.warn(`Missing style image: ${e.id}`);
  // Do NOT call map.addImage() here – it won’t resolve the request.
});
```

The event object only contains `{ id: string }` (plus the usual `type` and `target`).

---

## 5. Full minimal example (CDN ESM)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – dynamic missing icons</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    #map { position: absolute; top:0; bottom:0; left:0; right:0; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.mjs';

  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        osm: { type: 'raster', tiles: ['https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'], tileSize: 256 }
      },
      layers: [
        { id: 'raster', type: 'raster', source: 'osm' },
        {
          id: 'points',
          type: 'symbol',
          source: {
            type: 'geojson',
            data: {
              type: 'FeatureCollection',
              features: [
                { type: 'Feature', geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [0, 0] }, properties: { name: 'A' } },
                { type: 'Feature', geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [10, 0] }, properties: { name: 'B' } }
              ]
            }
          },
          layout: {
            // These IDs are *not* in any sprite sheet
            'icon-image': ['get', 'name'],
            'icon-size': 1.5,
            'text-field': ['get', 'name'],
            'text-offset': [0, 1.5]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [5, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });

  // ---- Resolver -------------------------------------------------
  map.setMissingStyleImageResolver((id) => {
    // Simple canvas icon – you could fetch instead
    const size = 64;
    const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');
    canvas.width = canvas.height = size;
    const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');
    ctx.fillStyle = '#ff6600';
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.arc(size / 2, size / 2, size / 2 - 4, 0, Math.PI * 2);
    ctx.fill();
    ctx.fillStyle = '#fff';
    ctx.font = '28px sans-serif';
    ctx.textAlign = 'center';
    ctx.textBaseline = 'middle';
    ctx.fillText(id[0].toUpperCase(), size / 2, size / 2);
    return canvas;   // ← resolves the missing-icon request
  });

  // Optional logging
  map.on('styleimagemissing', (e) => console.warn('Missing icon → resolver called for', e.id));
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the page and you’ll see two points, each rendered with a tiny orange circle whose first letter matches the feature’s `name` property – even though no sprite contains those icons.

---

## 6. Quick checklist

| ✅ What you need to do | ❌ What you must **not** do |
|------------------------|----------------------------|
| Call `map.setMissingStyleImageResolver()` **once**, before any `Map` is created (or at least before the first missing-icon request). | Call `map.addImage()` inside a `styleimagemissing` handler – it won’t resolve the request in v6. |
| Return a **synchronous** image source **or** a **Promise** that resolves to one. | Return `undefined` or a plain object that isn’t a valid image source (the map will treat it as “still missing”). |
| Keep the `styleimagemissing` listener only for diagnostics if you like. | Rely on the event to *fix* the missing icon. |
| Pin the MapLibre version (`@^6.0.0` or a concrete `6.x.y`) when using a CDN, because the default export and UMD bundle disappeared in v6. | Use the old default import (`import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl'`) – it throws a module-resolution error. |

---

### TL;DR code snippet

```js
// v6 import (named or namespace)
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

// … create the map …

// Register a resolver (sync or async)
map.setMissingStyleImageResolver((id) => {
  // e.g. fetch PNG → ImageBitmap, or generate a canvas
  return fetch(`/icons/${id}.png`)
           .then(r => r.blob())
           .then(b => createImageBitmap(b));
});
```

That’s all you need to dynamically supply icons at runtime in MapLibre GL JS v6. 🎉

Below is a **v6-compatible** way to read every piece of the camera state that the public API exposes, plus the only place you can still get the **projection matrix**.

---

## 1. Public getters (center, zoom, bearing, pitch)

```js
// anywhere after the map has been created
const center  = map.getCenter();   // → LngLat object
const zoom    = map.getZoom();     // → number
const bearing = map.getBearing(); // → number (degrees, clockwise)
const pitch   = map.getPitch();   // → number (degrees)

// If you prefer a plain object:
const camera = {
  lng:   center.lng,
  lat:   center.lat,
  zoom,
  bearing,
  pitch
};

console.log(camera);
```

All of these methods have existed since v5, and they **did not change** in v6.  
(If you are still on v5 you may have been reaching into the private `map.transform` object – that is no longer possible.)

---

## 2. Projection matrix – only inside a **custom layer**

The raw view-projection matrix is not exposed on `Map` any more.  
The only public hook that gives you the matrix is the `render` callback of a **custom layer**.  
MapLibre passes a `matrix` (a `Float32Array` of length 16) that you can use for your own math.

```js
// 1️⃣ Define a custom layer
const matrixLayer = {
  id: 'matrix-layer',
  type: 'custom',
  // Called once when the layer is added – you can set up shaders, buffers, etc.
  onAdd: function (map, gl) {
    // …your init code…
  },

  // Called every frame.  The `matrix` argument is the current projection matrix.
  render: function (gl, matrix) {
    // `matrix` is a column-major 4×4 matrix (Float32Array[16])
    // You can feed it to your own shader, or read its values here:
    console.log('Projection matrix:', matrix);

    // Example: compute the camera state again (optional)
    const center  = map.getCenter();
    const zoom    = map.getZoom();
    const bearing = map.getBearing();
    const pitch   = map.getPitch();

    // …do whatever custom math you need with `matrix` and the camera values…
  }
};

// 2️⃣ Add the layer to the map (after the map is ready)
map.on('load', () => {
  map.addLayer(matrixLayer);
});
```

### What the `matrix` actually is

* It combines **world → view → projection** transforms that MapLibre uses internally.
* It is already in the coordinate system expected by WebGL shaders (clip-space, `[-1, 1]` on X/Y/Z).
* If you need the **inverse** (e.g., screen → world), you can invert it with a small helper:

```js
function invertMatrix(m) {
  const inv = new Float32Array(16);
  // gl-matrix is a tiny, zero-dependency lib that does this safely:
  //   import { mat4 } from 'gl-matrix';
  //   mat4.invert(inv, m);
  // For brevity we just show the call signature.
  mat4.invert(inv, m);
  return inv;
}
```

(You can pull in `gl-matrix` or any other linear-algebra lib you like.)

---

## 3. “What if I’m not inside a custom layer?”

* **You cannot get the raw matrix directly** – the API deliberately hides it.
* If you only need to convert between geographic coordinates and screen pixels, use the public projection helpers:

```js
// Geographic → pixel (relative to the map container)
const pixel = map.project(center);   // → Point {x, y}

// Pixel → geographic
const lngLat = map.unproject(pixel); // → LngLat
```

* If you really need the exact matrix for some external calculation, create a tiny no-op custom layer (as shown above) whose sole purpose is to expose the matrix to the rest of your code (e.g., store it in a global variable or dispatch a custom event).

---

## 4. Quick recap (v6)

| Camera property | Public API                     |
|-----------------|--------------------------------|
| **center**      | `map.getCenter()` (LngLat)     |
| **zoom**        | `map.getZoom()`                |
| **bearing**     | `map.getBearing()`             |
| **pitch**       | `map.getPitch()`               |
| **projection matrix** | `matrix` argument of a **custom layer** `render()` callback |

If you are still on a v5 codebase and you see something like `map.transform.center` or `map.transform.matrix`, replace it with the pattern above – `map.transform` was removed in v6 and is no longer accessible.

--- 

### TL;DR

```js
// Camera state (always available)
const cam = {
  center:  map.getCenter(),
  zoom:    map.getZoom(),
  bearing: map.getBearing(),
  pitch:   map.getPitch()
};

// Projection matrix (only inside a custom layer)
map.addLayer({
  id: 'proj-matrix',
  type: 'custom',
  render: (gl, matrix) => {
    console.log('matrix =', matrix); // Float32Array[16]
    // …your custom math…
  }
});
```

That’s the complete, v6-compatible way to obtain the map’s camera state and its projection matrix. Happy coding!

Below is a **complete, type-safe** example you can drop into a MapLibre GL JS v6 project.  
It shows:

* the exact types you need to import (`MapSourceDataEvent` | `MapStyleDataEvent`);
* a **type-guard** that narrows the union to a source-event;
* a second guard that narrows the source-event further by its `sourceDataType` value;
* how to register the handler with `map.on('data', …)`.

```ts
/* -------------------------------------------------------------
   1️⃣  Imports – only the *type* exports are needed at runtime
   ------------------------------------------------------------- */
import type {
  MapSourceDataEvent,
  MapStyleDataEvent,
  // (optional, if you need the enum of possible sourceDataType strings)
  // SourceDataType,
} from 'maplibre-gl';

/* -------------------------------------------------------------
   2️⃣  Type-guards
   ------------------------------------------------------------- */

/**
 * Narrow a generic `data` event to a source-event.
 *
 * The `sourceDataType` property exists **only** on `MapSourceDataEvent`,
 * so the `'sourceDataType' in e` check is enough for the compiler.
 */
function isSourceDataEvent(
  e: MapSourceDataEvent | MapStyleDataEvent,
): e is MapSourceDataEvent {
  return 'sourceDataType' in e;
}

/**
 * Further narrow a source-event to a specific `sourceDataType`.
 *
 * You can add as many branches as you need – the example covers the
 * most common ones (`metadata`, `source`, `tile`, `visibility`).
 */
function handleSourceDataEvent(e: MapSourceDataEvent) {
  switch (e.sourceDataType) {
    case 'metadata':
      // Fired when the source’s metadata (e.g. bounds, minzoom, maxzoom) is ready.
      console.log('🔎 metadata ready for source', e.sourceId);
      break;

    case 'source':
      // The source itself has finished loading (e.g. GeoJSON parsed, vector tileset
      // manifest fetched). `e.data` contains the raw source data.
      console.log('📦 source loaded', e.sourceId, e.data);
      break;

    case 'tile':
      // A single tile has been loaded (or failed). `e.tile` holds the tile key.
      console.log(
        `🧱 tile ${e.tile?.z}/${e.tile?.x}/${e.tile?.y} ${e.tile?.status} for source`,
        e.sourceId,
      );
      break;

    case 'visibility':
      // The source’s visibility changed (e.g. a layer that uses it was toggled).
      console.log('👁️ visibility change for source', e.sourceId);
      break;

    default:
      // If MapLibre adds new sourceDataType values later, this branch will
      // remind you to handle them.
      console.warn('Unhandled sourceDataType:', (e as any).sourceDataType);
  }
}

/* -------------------------------------------------------------
   3️⃣  The actual `data`-event handler
   ------------------------------------------------------------- */
function onMapData(e: MapSourceDataEvent | MapStyleDataEvent) {
  if (isSourceDataEvent(e)) {
    // ✅ We now have a `MapSourceDataEvent` – safe to access source-specific props
    handleSourceDataEvent(e);
  } else {
    // This is a `MapStyleDataEvent` – style-related loading information.
    // Typical `sourceDataType` does **not** exist here.
    console.log('🖌️ style data event', e);
    // Example: you might want to know when the style JSON is fully loaded:
    if (e.dataType === 'style') {
      console.log('✅ style JSON loaded');
    }
  }
}

/* -------------------------------------------------------------
   4️⃣  Register the handler with your Map instance
   ------------------------------------------------------------- */
import { Map } from 'maplibre-gl'; // runtime import – the Map class itself

const map = new Map({
  container: 'map',               // id or HTMLElement
  style: 'https://example.com/style.json',
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2,
});

map.on('data', onMapData);
```

### What the code does

| Step | Why it matters for v6 |
|------|-----------------------|
| **Import only types** (`import type …`) | Keeps the bundle size unchanged – the types are erased at compile time. |
| **`isSourceDataEvent` guard** | `MapDataEvent` no longer exists; the union `MapSourceDataEvent | MapStyleDataEvent` must be narrowed before you can safely read `sourceDataType`. |
| **`switch (e.sourceDataType)`** | Gives you exhaustive, compile-time-checked handling of each possible `sourceDataType` value (`'metadata' | 'source' | 'tile' | 'visibility' | …`). |
| **Separate branch for `MapStyleDataEvent`** | Guarantees you never accidentally read `sourceId` or `sourceDataType` on a style-event, which would be a runtime error. |
| **`map.on('data', …)`** | The registration stays exactly the same as in v5; only the handler’s type signature changed. |

---

#### Quick checklist for a smooth migration

1. **Remove any `MapDataEvent` imports** – replace them with the two new types.
2. **Add the `isSourceDataEvent` guard** (or an equivalent `'sourceDataType' in e` check) before accessing source-specific fields.
3. **If you need to react to a particular `sourceDataType`,** use a `switch`/`if` as shown; the compiler will warn you if you miss a case.
4. **Keep your bundler configuration** (Vite, webpack, etc.) unchanged *except* for the `setWorkerUrl()` requirement – that is unrelated to the `data` event.

That’s it! Your `data` handler is now fully type-safe under MapLibre GL JS v6. 🎉

Below is a **minimal, working example** of a MapLibre GL JS v6 map inside a **Vite + TypeScript** project that was installed from npm.  
All the v6-specific breaking-change fixes are included and commented so you can see why they are needed.

---

## 1️⃣ Install the package (and the CSS)

```bash
# npm
npm i maplibre-gl@^6

# or yarn / pnpm
# yarn add maplibre-gl@^6
# pnpm add maplibre-gl@^6
```

> **Why the `@^6`?**  
> Pinning to the major version prevents the silent breakage that happened when the un-pinned CDN URL jumped from v5 → v6.

---

## 2️⃣ Vite config (optional, but recommended)

You don’t need any special Vite plugins for MapLibre GL JS, but you **must** allow the worker file to be imported as a URL.  
Vite already ships with the `?url` and `?worker&url` query-parameter handling, so no extra config is required.

If you have a custom `vite.config.ts` that restricts asset handling, make sure it includes `*.mjs` (the worker is an ES-module).

```ts
// vite.config.ts (only needed if you have a restrictive assetsInclude)
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';
import react from '@vitejs/plugin-react'; // or any other framework plugin

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [react()],
  // make sure Vite treats .mjs files as assets that can be imported as URLs
  assetsInclude: ['**/*.mjs'],
});
```

---

## 3️⃣ TypeScript entry point (e.g. `src/main.tsx` or `src/main.ts`)

```tsx
// src/main.tsx  (React example – drop the JSX if you’re not using React)
import React from 'react';
import ReactDOM from 'react-dom/client';

// ---- 1️⃣ Import MapLibre GL ----
// No default export any more → use a namespace or named imports
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import { setWorkerUrl } from 'maplibregl';

// ---- 2️⃣ Pull in the CSS (required for controls, popups, etc.) ----
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';

// ---- 3️⃣ Import the worker URL the Vite way ----
// `?worker&url` tells Vite to bundle the worker and give us a URL that
// points to the bundled file (including its own dependencies).
import workerUrl from 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl-worker.mjs?worker&url';

// ---- 4️⃣ Tell MapLibre where the worker lives (bundler-only step) ----
setWorkerUrl(workerUrl);

// ---- 5️⃣ Optional: Resolve missing style images at runtime ----
// In v6 `styleimagemissing` is notify-only. Use the new resolver API.
maplibregl.setMissingStyleImageResolver((id) => {
  // Example: generate a simple 16×16 red square on-the-fly.
  const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');
  canvas.width = canvas.height = 16;
  const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d')!;
  ctx.fillStyle = 'red';
  ctx.fillRect(0, 0, 16, 16);
  return canvas; // can also return a Promise<HTMLImageElement|ImageBitmap>
});

function App() {
  // Ref to the container div
  const mapContainer = React.useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);

  React.useEffect(() => {
    if (!mapContainer.current) return;

    // ---- 6️⃣ Create the map ----
    const map = new maplibregl.Map({
      container: mapContainer.current,
      // any style you like – here we use the public OSM style
      style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
      center: [0, 0], // [lng, lat]
      zoom: 2,
    });

    // ---- 7️⃣ Add a control (just to prove everything works) ----
    map.addControl(new maplibregl.NavigationControl());

    // ---- 8️⃣ Example of the new public camera API (instead of map.transform) ----
    map.on('load', () => {
      console.log('Zoom:', map.getZoom());
      console.log('Center:', map.getCenter().toArray());
    });

    // Clean up on unmount
    return () => {
      map.remove();
    };
  }, []);

  // The div that will host the map
  return <div ref={mapContainer} style={{ width: '100%', height: '100vh' }} />;
}

// ---- 9️⃣ Render the app (if you’re using React) ----
ReactDOM.createRoot(document.getElementById('root')!).render(<App />);
```

### If you’re **not** using React

