### TL;DR checklist for **Vite + TS + npm**  

1. `npm i maplibre-gl@^6`  
2. `import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl'` (no default)  
3. `import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css'`  
4. `import workerUrl from 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl-worker.mjs?worker&url'`  
5. `setWorkerUrl(workerUrl)` **once** before creating any `Map` instance  
6. (optional) `maplibregl.setMissingStyleImageResolver(...)` for dynamic icons  
7. Use the public camera API (`getZoom()`, `getCenter()`, …) – never `map.transform`  
8. If you type `data` events, import `MapSourceDataEvent | MapStyleDataEvent` instead of `MapDataEvent`.

That’s all you need to get a MapLibre GL JS v6 map running smoothly in a Vite + TypeScript environment. Happy mapping! 🚀

Below is a **complete, minimal example** that shows how to:

1. Load a **PMTiles** file (a single-file archive that contains vector tiles) in the browser.  
2. Register it as a **vector source** for MapLibre GL JS.  
3. Add a **line layer** that draws features from that source.

The code works with the current **v6** build of MapLibre GL JS (the ESM-only bundle).  
If you are still on v5 you can keep the same logic – just change the import style
(`import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl'` → `import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl'`).

---

## 1️⃣ Install / load the PMTiles helper library

MapLibre itself does not understand the `pmtiles://` protocol, so we use the
official helper from **@protomaps/pmtiles**.  
You can either:

* **npm / Yarn** (for a bundler-based project)

```bash
npm i maplibre-gl @protomaps/pmtiles
```

* **CDN** (for a quick demo)

```html
<script type="module" src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@protomaps/pmtiles@latest/dist/pmtiles.mjs"></script>
```

---

## 2️⃣ Full HTML + JS demo (CDN version)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>PMTiles → MapLibre line layer</title>
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>

  <!-- MapLibre GL JS v6 (ESM build) -->
  <script type="module">
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // 1️⃣ Import the libraries
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.mjs';
    import { PMTiles } from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@protomaps/pmtiles@latest/dist/pmtiles.mjs';

    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // 2️⃣ Register the pmtiles:// protocol with MapLibre
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // The PMTiles class implements the fetch-like interface that MapLibre
    // expects for custom protocols.
    const pmtiles = new PMTiles('https://YOUR_DOMAIN/path/to/your-data.pmtiles');

    // Tell MapLibre to use the PMTiles instance whenever it sees a URL
    // that starts with "pmtiles://".
    maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', (request) => {
      // `request.url` will be something like "pmtiles://my-data.pmtiles"
      // The PMTiles instance knows how to serve the needed tile bytes.
      return pmtiles.get(request);
    });

    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣ Create the map
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    const map = new maplibregl.Map({
      container: 'map',
      style: {
        version: 8,
        sources: {},          // we’ll add our source later
        layers: []            // we’ll add our layer later
      },
      center: [0, 0],         // lon, lat
      zoom: 2
    });

    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣ When the map is ready, add the vector source and line layer
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    map.on('load', () => {
      // ---- 4a️⃣ Add the vector source that points to the PMTiles file ----
      map.addSource('my-pmtiles-source', {
        type: 'vector',
        // The URL uses the custom protocol we just registered.
        // The part after the protocol can be any string – it is only used
        // as a key for the PMTiles instance.
        url: 'pmtiles://my-data.pmtiles'
      });

      // ---- 4b️⃣ Add a line layer that draws from a specific source-layer ----
      // Replace "my_layer_name" with the actual name of the layer inside the
      // vector tiles (you can inspect it with `maplibre-gl inspect` or by
      // opening the .pmtiles file with `pmtiles inspect`).
      map.addLayer({
        id: 'my-lines',
        type: 'line',
        source: 'my-pmtiles-source',
        'source-layer': 'my_layer_name',   // <-- change this!
        layout: {
          // optional: smooth line joins / caps
          'line-join': 'round',
          'line-cap': 'round'
        },
        paint: {
          'line-color': '#ff6600',
          'line-width': 3,
          'line-opacity': 0.9
        }
      });
    });
  </script>
</head>

<body>
  <div id="map"></div>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the code does

| Step | Explanation |
|------|-------------|
| **Import** | Pulls in the ESM build of MapLibre (`maplibregl`) and the `PMTiles` helper. |
| **Protocol registration** | `maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', …)` tells MapLibre how to fetch a tile when it sees a URL that starts with `pmtiles://`. The `PMTiles` instance reads the archive (via HTTP range requests) and returns the requested tile bytes. |
| **Map creation** | Starts a blank style (`{sources:{},layers:[]}`) so we can add our own source/layer programmatically. |
| **`map.on('load')`** | Guarantees the map’s internal style is ready before we inject our source and layer. |
| **`addSource`** | Declares a **vector** source whose `url` points to the custom protocol. |
| **`addLayer`** | Adds a **line** layer that reads from the source-layer inside the vector tiles. The `paint` block defines colour, width, etc. |

---

## 3️⃣ Using a bundler (npm / Yarn) – same logic, different imports

If you are building with Vite, Webpack, Rollup, etc., the code is almost identical:

```js
// src/main.ts (or .js)
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import { PMTiles } from '@protomaps/pmtiles';

// Register protocol (same as above)
const pmtiles = new PMTiles('/data/roads.pmtiles');
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', (req) => pmtiles.get(req));

// Create map
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: { version: 8, sources: {}, layers: [] },
  center: [-122.44, 37.76],
  zoom: 12
});

map.on('load', () => {
  map.addSource('roads', {
    type: 'vector',
    url: 'pmtiles://roads.pmtiles'
  });

  map.addLayer({
    id: 'roads-line',
    type: 'line',
    source: 'roads',
    'source-layer': 'road_centerline', // <-- actual layer name
    paint: {
      'line-color': '#0066ff',
      'line-width': 2
    }
  });
});
```

### Bundler-specific worker setup (v6)

When you use a bundler you **must** call `setWorkerUrl()` once before creating the map, otherwise the internal WebWorker that parses vector tiles cannot be located.

```js
import { Map, setWorkerUrl } from 'maplibre-gl';
import workerUrl from 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl-worker.mjs?worker&url';

// Tell MapLibre where the worker lives
setWorkerUrl(workerUrl);
```

(If you are using the CDN example above, **no** `setWorkerUrl()` call is needed.)

---

## 4️⃣ How to discover the correct `source-layer` name

The name you put in `'source-layer'` must match a layer that exists inside the
PMTiles archive. You can find it in a few ways:

* **Inspect the archive locally** with the CLI that ships with `pmtiles`:

  ```bash
  npx @protomaps/pmtiles inspect path/to/your-data.pmtiles
  ```

  The output lists all layer names.

* **Programmatically** after the map has loaded:

  ```js
  map.on('styledata', () => {
    const source = map.getSource('my-pmtiles-source');
    console.log('available layers:', source.vectorLayers);
  });
  ```

* **Open the file in a viewer** (e.g., [PMTiles Viewer](https://protomaps.github.io/PMTiles/)).

